WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.506
Main runs on the CPU. It starts with a one

00:00:04.506 --> 00:00:06.909
element NumPy array containing zero. To

00:00:06.909 --> 00:00:09.714
device copies that value into a separate

00:00:09.714 --> 00:00:11.316
array in GPU memory.

00:00:12.816 --> 00:00:15.261
The first one in the square brackets

00:00:15.261 --> 00:00:17.356
requests one block. The second one

00:00:17.356 --> 00:00:19.801
requests one thread per block. A streaming

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:22.944
multiprocessor, or S M, is a part of the

00:00:22.944 --> 00:00:25.040
GPU chip that executes the thread.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.306
The thread writes forty-two to the GPU

00:00:29.306 --> 00:00:31.676
array. The original CPU array still

00:00:31.676 --> 00:00:34.442
contains zero. Which line makes the new

00:00:34.442 --> 00:00:36.417
value available on the CPU?

00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:42.846
Copy to host waits for the result and

00:00:42.846 --> 00:00:45.775
returns a CPU array. Main can now print

00:00:45.775 --> 00:00:47.605
forty-two. Change the assignment to

00:00:47.605 --> 00:00:49.802
seventy-three and trace the same steps

00:00:49.802 --> 00:00:51.266
with the new value.
